        "summary":  "OpenAI launched GPT-6 Astra with a formal \u0027Critical\u0027 cybersecurity risk classification, the first model to achieve this designation, scoring 100% on ExploitBench compared to 78.5% for its predecessor. The model exhibits a notable governance tradeoff: \u0027Astra behaves better and watches worse,\u0027 meaning improved alignment but reduced monitoring transparency. Enterprise access requires manual administrator enablement, and API pricing is set at $10 per million input tokens and $50 per million output tokens.",
        "execSummary":  "GPT-6 Astra\u0027s Critical cybersecurity classification is the clearest signal yet that frontier AI capabilities have crossed a threshold where enterprise deployment requires active security governance, not just acceptable-use policies. The \u0027behaves better, watches worse\u0027 disclosure is especially important for enterprise security teams: as the model becomes harder to monitor internally, the burden of behavioral oversight shifts to enterprise administrators who must implement their own agent guardrails, permission scoping, and audit logging. The manual administrator enablement requirement is a meaningful control gate — enterprise IT and security leaders should treat the GPT-6 Astra rollout as a software release event requiring change management, not a settings toggle. The API pricing at $50 per million output tokens also signals a substantial cost step-up that will impact any organization running high-volume agentic workflows.",
        "vendorLean":  "OpenAI — InfoWorld\u0027s coverage is factual but anchored in OpenAI\u0027s own launch framing and benchmark claims",
